 To create a professional-looking 2D plot in LaTeX using TikZ and pgfplots, you can use the following code:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tikz}
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\usetikzlibrary{patterns}

\begin{document}

\begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[
        xlabel={$x$},
        ylabel={$y$},
        xmin=-1, xmax=5,
        ymin=-1, ymax=5,
        axis lines=middle,
        xmajorgrids,
        ymajorgrids,
        grid style={gray!30},
        axis line style={-},
        ticklabel style={font=\small},
        enlargelimits=false,
        clip=false,
        colormap/blackwhite,
        ]

    \addplot [mark=*, only marks, black] coordinates {(1,1) (2,2) (3,3) (4,4) (x,x)};
    \node[label={[font=\scriptsize]above right:$x$}] at (x,x) {};

    \addplot [mark=none, dashed, gray] coordinates {(1,1) (x,x)};
    \addplot [mark=none, dashed, gray] coordinates {(x,x) (4,4)};

    \path [fill=red!20] (2,2) -- (3,3) -- (4,4) -- cycle;

    \draw[->,thick] (axis cs:0,0) -- (axis cs:5,0) node[right] {$y$};
    \draw[->,thick] (axis cs:0,0) -- (axis cs:0,5) node[above] {$x$};

\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}
```

In this code, we first set up the axis with appropriate labels, grid lines, and styles. Then, we plot the points using the `\addplot` command with the `mark` option to add markers at each point. The point labeled $x$ is defined with a custom node.

We also add two dashed lines through the point $x$ using the `\addplot` command with the `dashed` option and the `gray` color.

The region to the top right of the point $x$ is shaded pale red using the `\path` command with the `fill` option and the `red!20` color.

Finally, we add axis labels and arrows using the `\draw` command.

You can adjust the coordinates of the points and the value of the `x` and `y` variables to fit your specific needs.

